        <body>
            <div xml:id="d473">

                <pb facs="0823" n="729" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg729"/>

                <head>No. 473.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Manning.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-05-25"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 25,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 107.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I transmit for your information, a copy of
                    a further letter from Messrs. Alexandre &amp; Sons, of the 23d instant, and
                    observe that the receipt now furnished and the accompanying explanation
                    establishes the fact, as alleged by those gentlemen, that the importing merchant
                    in Mexico directly receives the benefit of the 2 per cent. rebate in the
                    settlement of his account for duties, the merchant’s receipt being admitted as
                    part payment of the amount due on the goods.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>





